---
A support desk's ticket queue. The types opt in to an order with
`derives Ord`, which is all it takes for the generic code in
`triage.generic` and `bynk.list` to sort, rank and clamp them.
---
commons triage.tickets

uses bynk.list
uses triage.generic

---
How urgent a ticket is. An enum orders by variant position, so `Urgent` is
written first to make it the *smallest*: ascending order is then the order
to work in.
---
type Priority = enum { Urgent, Normal, Low } derives Ord

---
Who owns a ticket. A record of `String`s is a `Key` automatically, so it can
key a `Map` with nothing to declare. It `derives Ord` only because `Ticket`
does, and every field of an ordered record must be orderable.
---
type Team = {
	product: String,
	region: String,
} derives Ord

---
A ticket orders by its fields in declaration order: priority first, then
the oldest, then the id. Ids are unique, so `team` never decides anything.
Reordering these fields changes the queue.
---
type Ticket = {
	priority: Priority,
	--- Minutes since the shift started.
	opened: Int,
	id: String,
	team: Team,
} derives Ord

--- The ticket to pick up next: the smallest one, so `min`, not `max`.
fn nextUp(ts: List[Ticket]) -> Option[Ticket] {
	min(ts)
}

--- The whole queue, in working order.
fn worklist(ts: List[Ticket]) -> Sorted[Ticket] {
	Sorted.fromList(ts)
}

--- How many tickets are urgent.
fn urgentCount(ts: List[Ticket]) -> Int {
	occurrences(ts.map((t) => t.priority), Urgent)
}

---
The priority an automatic rule may set: never `Urgent`, which only a person
can choose. The generic `clamp` works on `Priority` because it `derives Ord`.
---
fn automatic(p: Priority) -> Priority {
	clamp(p, Normal, Low)
}

--- Open tickets per team, keyed by the `Team` record itself.
fn load(ts: List[Ticket]) -> Map[Team, Int] {
	countBy(ts, (t) => t.team)
}
